WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.137
Risk combines likelihood, impact, and context.

00:00:02.137 --> 00:00:05.531
A highly likely event with low impact may be manageable.

00:00:05.531 --> 00:00:09.265
A low-probability event with catastrophic impact may still deserve serious attention.

00:00:09.265 --> 00:00:11.302
Context decides how much risk matters.

00:00:11.302 --> 00:00:15.375
The same technical failure might be tolerable in a prototype and unacceptable

00:00:15.375 --> 00:00:17.072
in a production safety system.

00:00:17.072 --> 00:00:22.164
Expected value is a high-level way to think about outcomes by combining likelihood and consequence.

00:00:22.164 --> 00:00:27.256
If an option has a modest chance of a large benefit and a small downside,

00:00:27.256 --> 00:00:28.613
it may be attractive.

00:00:28.613 --> 00:00:32.687
If an option has a high chance of a small benefit and

00:00:32.687 --> 00:00:34.723
a meaningful chance of severe damage,

00:00:34.723 --> 00:00:36.081
it may not be.

00:00:36.081 --> 00:00:38.457
Expected value is not a magic formula,

00:00:38.457 --> 00:00:42.870
but it helps people avoid focusing only on the most emotionally vivid outcome.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.567
Average expectations are not enough.

00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:48.641
Tail risks are rare but severe outcomes that can dominate the decision.

00:00:48.641 --> 00:00:52.714
A plan may look fine on average but still expose the organization

00:00:52.714 --> 00:00:55.429
to a worst credible case it cannot absorb.

00:00:55.429 --> 00:00:56.448
Security, operations, safety,

00:00:56.448 --> 00:01:00.861
and continuity planning all require attention to what could happen at the edge,

00:01:00.861 --> 00:01:02.897
not just what is most likely.

00:01:02.897 --> 00:01:05.273
Fragile decisions fail sharply when conditions change.

00:01:05.273 --> 00:01:08.328
Resilient decisions preserve options, limit blast radius, include monitoring,

00:01:08.328 --> 00:01:09.686
and make recovery possible.

00:01:09.686 --> 00:01:11.383
Mitigation reduces likelihood or impact.

00:01:11.383 --> 00:01:14.099
Contingency planning prepares action if the risk materializes.

00:01:14.099 --> 00:01:18.172
Risk tolerance defines how much uncertainty and downside the decision maker is

00:01:18.172 --> 00:01:19.869
willing and able to accept.

00:01:19.869 --> 00:01:22.924
A responsible decision process considers upside, downside, and survivability.

00:01:22.924 --> 00:01:26.319
It asks what success looks like, what failure looks like,

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:29.034
what signals would show the situation is changing,

00:01:29.034 --> 00:01:32.089
and what action would be taken if assumptions break.

00:01:32.089 --> 00:01:34.126
Good risk thinking is not fear-based.

00:01:34.126 --> 00:01:37.860
It is a practical way to keep action aligned with consequences.
